{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeLuz2ioRXfBLhDZM2xLE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01F7odD4qL9csjVNiNra9qq7","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-01\\diagnostics\\curriculum-alignment.md","content":"# Curriculum-Alignment Audit — KCNA Chapter 1\n\n**Draft audited:** `.pipeline-state/ch-01/draft-v1.md` (voiced output; the voice stage wrote back to `draft-v1.md`, with `draft-v1-prevoice.md` as the pre-voice backup — there is no `draft-voice.md` on disk).\n**Authority:** cached snapshots in `../Book-KCNA/sources/` (84 files, all `fetched_at 2026-08-23`), cross-read against `.pipeline-state/ch-01/research-manifest.md`.\n**Chapter type:** `orientation` · `exam_domain: None` · `domain_weight_pct: 0`.\n\n**Verdict: PASS on objective coverage, with one blocking gap-handling failure inherited from the research stage.** The chapter claims zero exam objectives and correctly covers zero. Every *current*-blueprint fact in the draft validates cleanly against the cached snapshots. The failure is in §3's *retired*-blueprint column, which the research manifest explicitly ruled un-draftable and the draft drafted anyway.\n\n---\n\n## Objectives the outline claims to cover\n\nThe outline claims **none**. `exam_domain: \"None — orientation chapter, no domain objectives\"`, `domain_weight_pct: 0`, `kb_tags.objectives: []`, and all six body sections carry `objectives: []`.\n\nThis is correct against the authority. The KCNA blueprint (`lf-kcna-exam-page-2026-08-23`, `cncf-kcna-curriculum-pdf-2026-08-23`) defines four domains and eleven competencies, and **none of them is about exam mechanics, blueprint history, or study method.** An orientation chapter is by construction out-of-blueprint. There is no objective for it to under-cover.\n\n| Objective ID | Description | Covered in draft? | Depth |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| — | *No objective claimed by the outline* | n/a | n/a |\n\n**Coverage of the outline's own stated outcomes** (outline §2, retained there \"for the audit stages\") — these are not exam objectives, but they are what the chapter promised to deliver:\n\n| Outline outcome | Delivered? | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| Describe published format/duration/eligibility/validity; distinguish from commonly-reported figures | **partial** | §2 (lines 104–130) — mechanics correct; the published/unpublished split is mis-drawn. See Gap 2 |\n| Name the four domains + weights; state what each absorbed or shed on 2025-11-24 | **partial** | §3 (lines 142–189) — current column correct and sourced; retired column unsourced. See Gap 1 |\n| Detect whether third-party material targets the current or retired blueprint | YES | §3 (lines 195–199), a genuinely operational 15-second test |\n| Locate any chapter on the blueprint and any domain in the book | YES | §5 `ch01-fig02` (lines 302–319) |\n| Choose a reading path calibrated to your starting point | YES | §6 (lines 376–384) |\n\n---\n\n## Objectives covered in the draft but NOT in the outline\n\nDrift risk: material that expanded scope beyond the outline. Might be valuable, might be scope creep; flagged for author decision.\n\nAll four instances sit in the **Soundings answer key and §4** — precisely where the outline predicted the pressure would be, and posted an explicit warning against it (outline §3: *\"the temptation to teach the correct answer properly will be strong. Don't. Those are other chapters' Fixed Points, and spending them here costs the payoff twice.\"*). Three of four held. One did not.\n\n| # | Objective touched | Location | Assessment |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **D1 Containerization** — container shares the host kernel | Soundings A1, line 56 | **Within tolerance.** The outline sanctioned \"states the correct distinction in one line and cross-bears to Ch 2.\" The draft does exactly that and carries the cross-bearing. No action. |\n| 2 | **D1 Core Concepts** — orchestrator vs. runtime, *and the reconciliation loop* | Soundings A2, line 58 | **Drift — act.** \"Orchestrator vs. runtime\" is the sanctioned answer. But the clause *\"watches whether reality matches your declared intent, and corrects the difference\"* is the control-loop/reconciliation concept the outline reserves as **Ch 3's Fixed Point**. The outline's own spoiler check asserts Q2 does *not* give away Ch 3's control loop; this answer key makes that assertion false. |\n| 3 | **D4 Community and Collaboration** — Kubernetes governance | Soundings A3, line 60 | **Drift — act.** Delivers CNCF + Linux Foundation + 2015 Google donation + contributor scale, with two source tags. This is Ch 17 / D4.3 payload. Compounding problem: **A3 is the only Soundings answer with no cross-bearing.** The reader who misses it gets the answer but no destination — inverting the question's stated design purpose (\"tells such a reader they have a gap — five chapters before Ch 17 has to fix it\"). |\n| 4 | **D4 Ecosystem and Principles** — scope of \"cloud native\" | §4, line 281 | **Borderline — author's call.** The negative correction (\"does not mean runs in a public cloud\") is explicitly sanctioned. The two illustrations that follow — *\"A rack of hardware in your own building can be thoroughly cloud native. A fleet of rented cloud instances can fail every characteristic of the term\"* — are substantive positive claims about what the CNCF characteristics do and don't cover, in a section the outline says \"deliberately makes no citable factual claim about the CNCF definition\" and the manifest says \"must not acquire any [sources].\" They are unsourced by design and therefore unverifiable by the fact-accuracy stage. |\n\n**Not drift, explicitly cleared:** §3's naming of Prometheus, OpenTelemetry, GitOps, Helm and deployment strategies (lines 183–185) are signposts to where weight moved, not coverage. §1's scheduler/Pod mention (line 90) is an illustration of question *form*. Correct calls, all.\n\n**Structural withholding — working as designed.** §4 does **not** state the CNCF definition, and the plant-and-harvest structure the outline was anxious about (Open Question #4) survives intact. The Extended Analogy (lines 289–293) is used in the one section where the outline permits it, and nowhere else. This is the hardest thing the chapter had to do and it did it.\n\n---\n\n## Depth mismatches\n\nAn objective is \"covered\" if the draft addresses its learning outcome. \"Appropriate depth\" means coverage matches exam weight.\n\n**Weight-proportional analysis does not apply here** — every topic in this chapter carries 0% exam weight. The honest analog is the outline's declared depth band (`focused`) and its per-section plan, which is what the table measures.\n\n| Content area | Exam weight | Outline plan | Draft depth | Mismatch |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| §1 What the KCNA is | 0% | ⚪ brief, open curiosity gap | proportionate | OK |\n| §2 Published mechanics | 0% | ⚪ flat facts + one hazard | proportionate | OK |\n| §2 Pricing detail | 0% | \"state exam-only price with snapshot date, describe bundles without figures\" (Open Q#7; manifest concurs) | all three tiers, stated **three times** (Dead Reckoning line 104, Closer Look line 112, Summary line 449) | **over-covered** |\n| §3 Blueprint change | 0% | 🔵 chapter payload — deepest section | proportionate; correctly the densest section | OK |\n| §4 \"Cloud native\" plant | 0% | \"short by design\" | 2 min / ~430 words, incl. a 3-paragraph analogy | slightly over, acceptable |\n| §5 Book mechanics | 0% | ⚪ instrument panel | proportionate | OK |\n| §6 Reading paths | 0% | ⚪ calibration | proportionate | OK |\n\n### Two quantitative findings\n\n**1. The chapter runs long against its own depth band.** 6,629 words. The `focused` band is justified in the outline as \"short enough to actually read before Chapter 2.\" At a typical technical-reading rate (~200 wpm) the prose alone is ~33 minutes, before the 10 questions, two answer-key blocks, and two figures. Realistic completion is 45–50 minutes, not the 35 claimed. Not a correctness defect — the prose is dense rather than padded, and §3 earns its length — but the *self-reported* budget under-states it.\n\n**2. The Attention Budget table does not sum to its own total.** The nine section rows (lines 15–23) total **39 minutes**; the header (line 11) states **~35 minutes**. Straight arithmetic error, independent of finding 1.\n\n**Under-covered: nothing.** No content area is thinner than the outline specified.\n\n---\n\n## Gaps the research stage flagged\n\nThis is where the chapter's real problem is, and it is **not the drafting stage's fault.**\n\n**Root cause — a pipeline integrity failure.** The research stage could not write files (sandbox blocked `Write`; see `research-manifest.md` lines 1 and 9–13). It therefore emitted six snapshots to stdout in Appendix A — two *corrections* to existing files and four *new* files — and asked for them to be written before Stage 3. **They never were.** Verified on disk:\n\n- `sources/lf-kcna-exam-page-2026-08-23.md` — still `fetched_at: 22:30`, not the corrected `23:50` re-fetch.\n- `sources/lf-kcna-program-changes-2026-08-23.md` — still `fetched_at: 22:30`, and **still contains the misattributed `46%` retired weights** the correction was written to remove.\n- `lf-mc-exam-faq-*`, `cncf-kcna-certification-page-*`, `cncf-curriculum-repo-kcna-versions-*`, `provenance-kcna-60-questions-*`, `cncf-kcna-curriculum-retired-*` — **all absent** (84 snapshots on disk; none of these six).\n\nSo the drafting stage read a stale, known-bad snapshot set and drafted faithfully from it. Its citations *do* validate against the files as they currently sit. Fixing the draft without fixing `sources/` will regress on the next run.\n\n| Gap (manifest) | Manifest status | Draft handling | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Retired five-domain weights** | **OPEN — blocking `ch01-fig01`** | Drafted in full | **NOT handled — blocking** |\n| **Passing score is published (75%)** | **Correction #1 — claim reversed** | Pre-correction framing retained | **NOT handled** |\n| **Question count** | **CLOSED in the book's favour** | Handled correctly | **OK** |\n| **G31 — cert ladder** | **CLOSED, resolves to option (a)** | Took option (b) | **Under-used** |\n| **G37 — LFS250 syllabus** | Partially closed, no effect | Correctly unaffected | **OK** |\n| **Scoring methodology** | Open, non-blocking | Not raised | **OK — and fortunate** |\n\n### Gap 1 — retired blueprint weights: drafted against an explicit prohibition\n\nThe manifest (finding 3, lines 71–84) could not source the retired weights and wrote: *\"**DO NOT draft the retired weights from memory or from third-party study guides**\"* and *\"§3 can state the structural change (five domains to four, observability folded into Cloud Native Architecture) from the LF page, but **not the retired percentages**, and `ch01-fig01` cannot be specced.\"*\n\nThe draft states all five (line 159), attributed to `lf-kcna-program-changes-2026-08-23` — the exact snapshot the manifest corrected *because the LF page never contained them*. The unsourced figures then propagate through six further constructions:\n\n- `ch01-fig01`'s entire left column and all three delta annotations, −2 / +6 / ×2 (lines 163–177)\n- the three \"what changed\" claims (lines 183–189)\n- the 🪝 Snag on Architecture 16→12 (line 191)\n- the \"under-serve Application Delivery by roughly half\" test (line 197)\n- the Logbook Entry's whole premise — an 8% standalone observability domain (lines 386–392)\n- the Chapter Summary row (line 452)\n\nOnly the *structural* half of §3 is currently supportable: five domains → four, and observability folded into Cloud Native Architecture. Both are directly quotable from the LF page.\n\nThe manifest also flags that the re-fetch recovered material §3 should have but doesn't: the rule that **the only date that matters is the date you sit the exam** (not purchase date, not first-attempt-vs-retake). That is the precise reader-facing consequence §3 exists to deliver, and it is absent from the draft.\n\n### Gap 2 — the passing score: the draft asserts the reverse of the finding\n\nManifest finding 1 (lines 41–55) establishes that the LF *does* publish a passing score, verbatim, in its candidate handbook: *\"A score of 75% or above must be earned to pass the Multiple Choice Exam\"* (`docs.linuxfoundation.org/tc-docs/certification/faq-mc`). Snapshot A3 lists the exact sentence to avoid: *\"FALSE, do not write: 'The Linux Foundation does not publish a passing score.'\"*\n\nThe draft writes materially that sentence three times:\n\n- line 116 — \"**The Linux Foundation does not publish a question count or a passing score for the KCNA.**\" (bolded; the section's load-bearing claim)\n- line 118 — both figures \"not published by the certifying body\"\n- line 254 — \"The widely-repeated '75% to pass' is a third-party report.\"\n\n**Owning stage:** fact-accuracy. Logged here because it determines whether the draft handled a research finding correctly, and it does not.\n\n**Two things survive and should not be touched.** ☆ Bearings #1 Q2 (line 222) was narrowed to *\"not published by the Linux Foundation **on the exam page**\"* — which is exactly the disambiguation the manifest asked for (line 55), and which makes C uniquely correct, since duration, attempts and weights are all on that page and the passing score is not. The stem and its explanation (line 248) are sound as written. And the manifest's sharper framing is *better* than the draft's: the two numbers have **different provenance** — one published in a handbook most candidates never read, one published nowhere. The ⚠ Navigational Hazards block survives intact and gains a worked contrast.\n\n### Gap 3 — question count: correctly handled\n\nSix authoritative sources checked, none states it. The draft's claim is not merely unrefuted but positively well-evidenced. No change.\n\n### Gap 4 — G31 cert ladder: closed, under-used\n\nThe outline recommended option (b) — name only CKA — *\"unless the research stage returns the catalog cheaply.\"* It did (manifest lines 98–106): CNCF's own KCNA page states KCNA prepares candidates to *\"pursue further CNCF credentials, including CKA, CKAD, and CKS,\"* and supplies the multiple-choice-vs-performance-based contrast that §1's \"design decision, not a lesser version\" argument is built on. §1 names only CKA (line 94). Not an error — the CKA citation is sound — but the trigger condition for option (a) was met and the draft didn't take it. Author's call. **KCSA still must not be named here.**\n\n### Gap 5 — scoring methodology: correctly avoided\n\nThe outline offered an optional 🔭 Closer Look on \"why a certifying body might decline to publish a passing score.\" The draft repurposed its Closer Look to pricing volatility (line 112) instead. Given Gap 2, that speculation would now have been built on a false premise. Fortunate, and worth preserving as-is.\n\n---\n\n## Recommended fixes\n\nFor the revision stage, in dependency order. **Fix 0 gates 1 and 2** — do it first or the rest regresses.\n\n**0. Land the research stage's snapshots before revising prose.** Write Appendix A of `research-manifest.md` to `../Book-KCNA/sources/`: replace `lf-kcna-exam-page-2026-08-23.md` (A1) and `lf-kcna-program-changes-2026-08-23.md` (A2); add `lf-mc-exam-faq-2026-08-23.md` (A3), `cncf-kcna-certification-page-2026-08-23.md` (A4), `cncf-curriculum-repo-kcna-versions-2026-08-23.md` (A5), `provenance-kcna-60-questions-2026-08-23.md` (A6). A6 is evidence, **not** a citable source — never cite it as fact. Then retrieve `https://github.com/cncf/curriculum/raw/master/old-versions/KCNA_Curriculum%20old.pdf` and record its domain list as `cncf-kcna-curriculum-retired-2026-08-23.md`. That last step is the two-minute manual action the manifest asked for and is the only thing standing between §3 and correctness.\n\n**1. §3 + `ch01-fig01` — resolve the retired column (blocking).** If fix 0's PDF retrieval succeeds, re-cite lines 159, 183–189, 191, 197, 452 to `cncf-kcna-curriculum-retired-2026-08-23` and the figure is sound as drawn. If it does not, rewrite §3 to the structural change only — five domains → four, observability folded into Architecture, both quotable from the LF page — drop all five percentages and the −2/+6/×2 annotations, and respec `ch01-fig01` as the one-sided \"what moved\" diagram the manifest names as the fallback. The Logbook Entry needs the same treatment: its premise survives qualitatively (\"a standalone observability domain, and Application Delivery weighted far lower\") without the 8%/16% figures. **Do not** source the weights from the CNCF-hosted Medium repost — it is a syndicated guest post, which is precisely the diffusion mechanism §2 teaches readers to catch.\n\n**2. §2 — re-cut the published/unpublished split (lines 116, 118, 254).** Replace \"does not publish a question count or a passing score\" with the two-provenance framing: the passing score **is** published — 75%, in the LF's multiple-choice candidate FAQ, not on the exam page most candidates read; the question count is published **nowhere**, across six authoritative sources. Cite A3. The hazard sharpens rather than weakens: the reader now has a published number and an inherited number side by side, which is a better teaching instrument than two absences. Leave ☆ Bearings #1 Q2 and its explanation alone — the \"on the exam page\" narrowing already makes them correct.\n\n**3. §3 — add the sit-date rule.** Insert the LF's rule after line 189: neither purchase date nor first-attempt-vs-retake matters; the only date that counts is the date you sit. Cite A2. This is the reader-facing consequence §3 was built to deliver and it is currently missing.\n\n**4. Soundings A2 (line 58) — stop short of the control loop.** Cut *\"watches whether reality matches your declared intent, and corrects the difference.\"* Keep orchestrator-vs-runtime and the existing Ch 3 cross-bearing. The reconciliation loop is Ch 3's Fixed Point; naming it here spends it twice.\n\n**5. Soundings A3 (line 60) — trim to calibration, add the missing cross-bearing.** Keep the one-line answer (an open-source foundation — the CNCF, part of the Linux Foundation). Cut the 2015 Google donation and the contributor-scale figures; that is Ch 17's D4.3 material and this is the competency B1 flags as most under-studied, so it should arrive fresh. Add `*[cross-bearing: see Ch 17 §2 — CNCF governance and the project lifecycle]*` — A3 is the only answer in the set without one, and this question's whole design purpose is to route the reader forward.\n\n**6. §2 — collapse the pricing repetition.** State $250 exam-only with its 2026-08-23 snapshot date; name the two bundles without figures. Drop the tier prices from the Dead Reckoning block (line 104) and the Summary row (line 449); the 🔭 Closer Look on volatility (line 112) already covers the caveat and should stay. This is the outline's Open Q#7 recommendation, which the manifest endorsed.\n\n**7. Attention Budget — fix the arithmetic and the band.** Section rows sum to 39, header says ~35. Reconcile, and raise the total to ~45 minutes to account for the 10 questions and two answer-key blocks the reading estimate omits. Optionally move Soundings out of the total, since a reader who skips them genuinely spends less.\n\n**8. §4 (line 281) — author's call on the two illustrations.** The on-prem/rented-cloud pair is rhetorically effective and makes the correction concrete, but they are positive claims about the CNCF characteristics in a section barred from carrying a source. Either soften to the negative correction alone (\"it is not a statement about *where* the system runs\"), or accept them as authored framing and note the exemption for the fact-accuracy stage. Recommend softening — the section's power is what it withholds, and these sentences edge toward the definition.\n\n**9. Optional — §5/Bearings #1 A3 (line 258): state the deviation.** The draft says chapter allocation deviates from published weights \"by a small margin.\" The outline's figure is **max 2.8 points**, and it checks out: across the 17 content chapters, Part II is 41.2% vs 44% (−2.8), Part III 29.4% vs 28%, Part IV 17.6% vs 16%, Part V 11.8% vs 12%. Stating 2.8 makes the disclosure checkable, which is the habit §2 spends a page building.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5881,"cache_read_input_tokens":166555,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5881},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bcbbddf9-5927-43db-8fe2-6f0f3f3572ca","uuid":"1137c532-9259-4613-ad4e-c00993c55631","timestamp":"2026-08-24T04:08:53.543Z","request_id":"req_011CeLuyzWbXNbMMBjjWBXRd"}
